   "op": "engine",
   "note": "Transliteration (⟶) of English prose drops unvocalised Arabic and converts ؟ to ?, losing the very example the sentence shows. Keep an unvocalised Arabic word in the ⟶ line (as-is, or as 'أنت (ʾnt)') and keep the Arabic question mark ؟ when it is quoted in prose. Examples: grammar pronouns (explanation) 'Without harakat both are written , so…' → 'Without harakat both are written أنت (ʾnt), so…'; grammar questions (explanation) 'The Arabic question mark ? is a mirrored ?.' → 'The Arabic question mark ؟ is a mirrored ?.' (a ؟ that ends an Arabic question, e.g. hal anta miṣrī?, may still become ?)."
  },
  {
   "chunk": "grammar",
   "ids": [
    28
   ],
   "op": "engine",
   "note": "The s·h separator (·) is inserted after a tatweel / hyphen: bare object endings ـهُ ـهَا ـهُمْ come out -·hu, -·hā, -·hum. Insert · only after a real vowelless s/k/d/t + h (As·halu). Examples: grammar object-pronouns-w9 formula 'verb + -nī · -ka · -ki · -·hu · -·hā · -nā · -kum · -·hum' → '… -hu · -hā · -nā · -kum · -hum'; its explanation 'with -·hu for a masculine word and -·hā' → '-hu … -hā'; glossary-hand ـهُ ⟶ -·hu → -hu. Same root cause as glossary-hand id 1 (one fix)."
  },
  {
   "chunk": "grammar",
   "ids": [
    29
   ],
   "op": "engine",
   "note": "One clitic style not applied everywhere: proclitics wa-/fa-/li-/la-/bi- stay joined in some words. Hyphenate consistently. Examples (grammar): وَجُبْنًا 'wajubnan' → 'wa-jubnan' (next to wa-khubzan); لِتَصْنَعَ 'litaṣnaʿa' → 'li-taṣnaʿa'; فَسَنَزُورُ 'fasanazūru' → 'fa-sanazūru'; لَسَافَرْتُ 'lasāfartu' → 'la-sāfartu'; بِقَبُولِ 'biqabūli' → 'bi-qabūli'; 75 'khamsa wasabʿūn' → 'khamsa wa-sabʿūn'. Reviewer A's glossary-hand note shows the same misses: بِنَعْنَاع Binaʿnāʿ, لِيَشْتَرِيَ Liyashtariya, بِأَنَّ Biʾanna."
  },
  {
   "chunk": "glossary-hand",
   "ids": [
    1
   ],
   "op": "engine",
   "note": "Same problem as the grammar id 28 engine op (no separate work): do not insert the · separator when ه follows a tatweel or starts the fragment. ـهُ ⟶ -hu, ـهَا ⟶ -hā, ـهُمْ ⟶ -hum; keep As·halu."
  },
